// JSON "reform" file that specifies the changes in current-law policy
// required to make Tax-Calculator use the same defensible conventions
// as policyengine-taxsim (the pinned version in pe_pin.json).
// Every entry is explained below; see VARIABLES.md for details.
//
// (1) eitc_claim_prob_scale = 9e99 (rather than 1.03)
// (2) actc_claim_prob_scale = 9e99 (rather than 1.1)
//     By default Tax-Calculator models incomplete take-up of the EITC
//     and the refundable child tax credit by randomly zeroing some
//     credit amounts (using the credit_claim_urn variable).  PE computes
//     credit entitlement with full take-up (takes_up_eitc defaults to
//     True), so these two parameters are set to 9e99, the documented
//     value that implies every filing unit claims its credit.
//
// (3) SS_thd1 = SS_thd2 = 0 for married filing separately units
//     (rather than 25000 and 34000)
//     Tax-Calculator has no lived-apart-all-year flag and so treats
//     every MARS=3 unit as having lived apart from the spouse all year,
//     which gives the separate filer the single filer's base amounts
//     in the taxable Social Security benefits worksheet.  PE treats
//     every separate filer as having lived with the spouse, for which
//     the worksheet base amounts are zero, so up to 85 percent of
//     benefits are taxable.  These values make TC do the same.
//
{
    "eitc_claim_prob_scale": {"2013": 9e99},
    "actc_claim_prob_scale": {"2013": 9e99},
    "SS_thd1": {"2013": [25000, 32000, 0, 25000, 25000]},
    "SS_thd2": {"2013": [34000, 44000, 0, 34000, 34000]}
}
